    3. operator_fatigue (hard_constraint) vs. resource_efficiency (maximize)
       Q: At what throughput multiplier relative to today's baseline does operator fatigue typically reach the 0.4 ceiling — and if the optimiser hits that ceiling early in the shift, should it hold throughput steady for the remainder or throttle back to allow fatigue recovery?
       A: Once the fatigue index reaches 0.38 (95 % of the hard limit), throughput is capped at the level that produced that reading; no active recovery strategy is applied, so the constraint acts as a one-way ceiling for the rest of the shift.
    4. noise_level / noise_measurement
       Q: Does the 80 dB limit refer to the LAeq time-average over the full shift (as assumed), or to a shorter-window rolling average (e.g., any 15-minute period) or an absolute instantaneous peak, given that a shift-average of 80 dB is compatible with short bursts well above 85 dB?
       A: The constraint is applied as a 15-minute rolling LAeq window (≤ 80 dB at all times during the shift) rather than a single shift-wide average, which is more protective and consistent with ISO 9612 occupational noise standards.
    5. context / product_variant_mix
       Q: Is the mix of product variants to be produced this shift fixed by customer orders (so the optimiser controls only pacing and sequencing), or is the variant mix itself an optimisable decision variable that the optimiser can shift toward higher-value items to raise the efficiency ratio?
       A: Variant mix is treated as fixed by the pre-shift production order; the optimiser controls only task sequencing, pacing, and resource allocation, not what is produced.

