       A: Assume a standard 8-hour shift. Fatigue normalisation is calibrated to 480 minutes of continuous exposure. If the actual shift is longer, the 0.4 ceiling may be unreachable without mandatory breaks, and the scheduler should insert at least one 30-minute rest period.
    2. value_per_resource_consumed (maximize, threshold: null) — conflict with resource_scope including labor
       Q: If the optimiser reduces headcount (fewer operators) to cut the labor component of resource consumption, each remaining operator bears a higher workload. Should labor headcount be held fixed for this shift, or is headcount a free decision variable — and if it is free, what is the minimum staffing level?
       A: Hold operator headcount fixed at the shift baseline. Labor cost is included in the resource denominator for accounting purposes only; the scheduler may not reduce staffing below the current roster. This prevents fatigue from spiking as a side-effect of cost optimisation.
    3. value_per_resource_consumed (maximize, threshold: null) — no minimum throughput floor
       Q: Without a minimum delivered-value floor, the ratio can be mathematically satisfied by near-idle operation (very low numerator and even lower denominator). What is the minimum acceptable delivered value per shift (in currency units) or minimum units produced, so that gaming the ratio by throttling output is ruled out?
       A: Apply a soft lower bound equal to 90% of the rolling 5-shift average delivered value. If no historical data is available, use 85% of nameplate capacity as the floor. Schedules delivering less than this are penalised before ratio optimisation begins.
    4. noise_level (threshold: 80.0 dB, measurement: time_weighted_average)
       Q: The 80 dB constraint is expressed as a time-weighted average. Peak impulse events (e.g., press cycles at 95 dB for 0.5 s) can comply with an 80 dB TWA while still exceeding regulatory peak-exposure limits (typically 135 dB C-weighted). Should a separate instantaneous peak cap (e.g., 85 dB or 110 dB peak) also be enforced?
