       Q: Is there a minimum delivered-value target (production quota) for the shift — for example, at least 800 units or £12 000 of output — below which the shift is considered a failure regardless of efficiency ratio? Without a floor the optimiser can legally maximise the value/resource ratio by producing a single high-margin item and idling, which would not meet actual production commitments.
       A: Apply a minimum throughput floor equal to 90 % of the rolling 30-shift average delivered value for this shift type; if historical data are unavailable, set the floor to the contractual customer-order volume for the shift.
    4. noise_level — measurement semantics (instantaneous vs. LAeq)
       Q: Does the 80 dB(A) limit apply as a continuous instantaneous ceiling (no reading may exceed 80 dB(A) at any moment) or as a time-averaged LAeq over the full shift per ISO 9612? Cyclic machines commonly generate peaks of 85–90 dB(A) that average below 80 dB(A); the choice determines which equipment-selection and sequencing options remain feasible.
       A: Enforce 80 dB(A) as the shift-level LAeq per ISO 9612, consistent with the assumption already stated in the contract, while additionally rejecting any plan that produces a predicted instantaneous peak above 90 dB(A) (EU Directive 2003/10/EC upper exposure action value).
    5. context.temporal_horizon — shift duration unspecified
       Q: What is the planned duration of this shift (e.g. 8 h, 10 h, 12 h)? Shift length directly calibrates the fatigue-accumulation trajectory (a 0.4 fatigue index reached after 6 h implies a different work-intensity profile than after 10 h), the LAeq noise average, and whether intra-shift rolling re-optimisation windows are wide enough to be meaningful.
       A: Assume a standard 8-hour shift (480 minutes) with a 30-minute unpaid break excluded from fatigue and noise accumulation; flag the assumption in the generated schedule header so the manager can correct it before execution.

