    2. delivered_value_per_resource_consumed / kpi_targets[0].threshold
       Q: Is there a minimum acceptable absolute throughput volume (e.g., at least N conforming units or Y value-units delivered) below which a high efficiency ratio is still considered a failure — because without a floor the optimiser may run one very-high-value, very-low-resource job and declare success while leaving most capacity idle?
       A: A minimum absolute delivered-value floor equal to 80% of the historical shift baseline is enforced as an additional hard constraint, preventing degenerate low-volume high-ratio solutions.
    3. context.horizon / shift_duration
       Q: What is the exact duration of this shift in hours (e.g., 8 h, 10 h, 12 h), and does that duration include scheduled breaks — because fatigue accumulation rates, permissible noise exposure dose (dB × hours), and the resource-consumption baseline all scale with clock time, so an unspecified horizon makes those limits meaningless?
       A: Shift duration is assumed to be 8 hours of active production time, excluding scheduled breaks; fatigue and noise constraints are evaluated over that 8-hour window.
    4. operator_fatigue_index / fatigue_measurement
       Q: At what frequency is the fatigue index updated and checked against the 0.4 hard-constraint limit during the shift (e.g., every 15 minutes, every hour, continuously) — because a coarse update interval allows the index to breach the limit between readings, and the corrective-action latency changes what cycle-rate policies are actually safe?
       A: Fatigue index is evaluated every 15 minutes; any projected breach triggers a mandatory pace-reduction action before the next interval, and the hard constraint is treated as binding at each 15-minute checkpoint rather than only at end-of-shift.
    5. ambient_noise_level / noise_measurement
       Q: Is the 80 dB LAeq limit computed over the full shift as a single time-average, or over shorter rolling windows (e.g., 15-minute or 1-hour LAeq) — because a full-shift average permits loud production bursts early in the shift that a rolling-window limit would forbid, substantially changing which machine schedules are feasible?
       A: The 80 dB limit is applied as a 1-hour rolling LAeq so that sustained loud periods cannot be masked by quiet periods elsewhere in the shift, consistent with ISO 9612 occupational noise assessment practice.

