    A 20% demand surge has produced a schedule that violates two human-centric constraints simultaneously. The FatigueIndex reached 0.6001 (limit 0.40, excess +0.2001) and the Noise level reached 84.9 dB (limit 80.0 dB, excess +4.9 dB). Because both violations involve worker health and safety, the schedule was automatically rejected by the CBPA governance layer and requires production manager authorization before any remediation path is enacted. No automated relaxation has been applied. Ranked options below progress from full constraint preservation to partial relaxation with compensatory controls.

  [Escalation] Remediation options presented to manager:
    - Option 1 — Add Capacity via Rotating Micro-Shifts (Recommended): Introduce two additional 4-hour micro-shifts per day staffed by cross-trained workers drawn from adjacent cells or temporary agency labour. Spreading the increased demand across more workers and more clock-hours keeps individual FatigueIndex below 0.40 and allows acoustic rest intervals that hold average Noise exposure below 80 dB. No existing constraint limit is changed. Requires HR coordination and a 24–48 h ramp-up lead time before the revised schedule can execute.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 2 — Defer 12% of Demand to Next Planning Cycle: Accept fulfilment of 88% of the incremental demand in the current cycle and reschedule the remaining 12% to the next production window. The reduced load brings the projected FatigueIndex to 0.38 and Noise to 79.1 dB, both safely within limits. Requires customer or supply-chain negotiation to confirm acceptability of the partial shipment. No constraint is relaxed; the human-centric limits are fully honoured at the cost of a controlled delivery gap.
      Preserves human constraints: Yes
    - Option 3 — Targeted Noise Relaxation to 83 dB with Mandatory PPE Protocol (Last Resort): Raise the Noise ceiling from 80.0 dB to 83.0 dB for the duration of the elevated-demand period only. Simultaneously redistribute task sequences to cap the FatigueIndex at 0.40 (no relaxation on fatigue). The 3 dB relaxation must be accompanied by: mandatory Class-B hearing-protection equipment for all affected workers, acoustic-exposure monitoring logged every 30 minutes, and automatic reversion to 80 dB once demand returns to baseline. This option meets the full 20% demand surge but requires formal manager sign-off, an updated risk register entry, and occupational-health notification under site safety policy. The FatigueIndex constraint is preserved; only the Noise limit is temporarily relaxed.
      Preserves human constraints: No

